<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shahrisabz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shahrisabz is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashkadarya">Kashkadarya</a> valley. Known in earlier times as Kesh, it was a Sogdian principality, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> was born nearby in 1336. He built there the vast Ak-Saray palace, whose ruined portal still stands, and the town's historic centre is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shahrisabz.jpg" alt="Shahrisabz" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Normuratov7 · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amir_Temur_Statue_and_Ak-Saray_Monument_in_Shahrisabz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shahrisabz lies about 80 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> at an elevation of 622 m, in Qashqadaryo Region. Historically it was called Kesh or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, meaning heart-pleasing, and has been known as Shahrisabz since the Timurid era. It is one of the most ancient cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and was founded more than 2,700 years ago. It has been tentatively identified with the ancient Nautaca, and excavated hills near the village of Kumyrtepa in the Kitab district are held to be parts of that city: a citadel, the town and a Zoroastrian temple, among the oldest religious structures in Central Asia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kesh was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> from the 6th to the 4th century BCE and an important urban centre of Sogdiana. Alexander the Great spent winters in the area and met his wife Roxanna there around 328 to 327 BCE. From the 6th to the 8th century Kesh belonged to the Turkic and Western Turkic khaganates.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> conquered it in the 8th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashkadarya">Kashkadarya</a> valley became the centre of a revolt led by al-Muqanna, known as the Revolt of the Men in White Clothes. The Karakhanid Ibrahim ibn Nasr conquered Kesh in 1040, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmshah">Khwarazmshahs</a> in the early 13th century it was first enclosed by defensive walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> was born at Kesh into a family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barlas">Barlas</a>, and in the early years of the Timurid dynasty the city enjoyed his patronage. He regarded it as his home town and meant it to hold his tomb, but under his reign the centre of activity shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p><p>Work on the Ak-Saray palace began in 1380, with artisans deported from Khwarezm; only parts of its 65 m gate-towers survive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg">Ulugh Beg</a> built the Kok Gumbaz Mosque in 1437 in honour of his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-rukh">Shah Rukh</a>. The Dorus-Saodat complex holds the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>'s eldest son Jehangir. A stone casket intended for Timur was found in a crypt in 1943, although he was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. The historic centre was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2000, and the city's population was 152,700 in 2026.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shahrisabz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Shahrisabz</b>, the old city of Kesh and birthplace of Timur, was the stronghold of the Kenagas Uzbek tribe, which long resisted the Manghit Emirate of Bukhara.</blockquote><a href="/state/beylik-of-shahrisabz">Beylik of Shahrisabz</a></li><li><blockquote>The city, renamed <b>Shahrisabz</b>, later became the home town of Timur.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kesh">Principality of Kesh</a></li></ul></section>
